{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Abm5wVsc1XHsvfzzoUWtsx","type":"tool_result","content":"   620→            <section>\n   621→                <h2>📚 Book Structure</h2>\n   622→                \n   623→                <p style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8;\">This book contains 25 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.</p>\n   624→                \n   625→                \n   626→            </section>\n   627→\n   628→            \n   629→            <section>\n   630→                <h2>📖 Plot Summary</h2>\n   631→                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">My creator, Victor Frankenstein, recounts his tragic life from childhood in Geneva, where he was raised alongside his beloved adoptive sister Elizabeth Lavenza and his close friend Henry Clerval. His early fascination with alchemy and the mysteries of life, sparked by forbidden texts and a thunderstorm’s revelation of natural forces, led him to the University of Ingolstadt, where he became obsessed with the science of life itself. Driven by an insatiable ambition to transcend human limits, he secretly constructed a sentient creature from assembled body parts, only to recoil in horror at its grotesque form upon its animation. Abandoning his creation in terror, he fell into a severe illness, sustained only by Clerval’s unwavering loyalty during his recovery.\n   632→\n   633→After returning to Geneva, Victor is devastated by the murder of his younger brother William, falsely blamed on their servant Justine Moritz, whom he knows to be innocent. Consumed by guilt, he confronts the creature, who reveals himself as a tormented outcast, abandoned and rejected by all. The creature demands a companion to alleviate his suffering, and after agonizing moral struggle, my creator reluctantly agrees, fleeing to the remote Orkney Islands to begin the work. However, overcome by dread at the prospect of creating another being like his first, he destroys the unfinished female creature. The enraged monster vows vengeance, specifically threatening to be present on my creator’s wedding night.\n   634→\n   635→The wedding to Elizabeth, a union my creator undertakes in desperate hope to end his torment, becomes the final tragedy. On their wedding night, the creature murders Elizabeth, leaving my creator shattered and consumed by grief. His father dies of sorrow, and he is falsely accused of murdering Clerval, who was killed by the creature in Ireland. After being exonerated, he pursues the monster across Europe and into the Arctic, driven by a vow of vengeance. In the frozen wastes, he finally catches sight of the creature’s sledge, but a seismic rift separates them. As he drifts on a melting ice floe, he is rescued by Robert Walton’s expedition. Before dying, my creator implores Walton to continue the hunt, recounting his entire story in a final act of warning. The creature appears at the end, remorseful and broken, confessing his descent into evil due to isolation and rejection, and then vanishes into the ice, ending his own life on a pyre he has built.</div>\n   636→                \n   637→                <p style=\"margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted);\">\n   638→                    <strong>Themes:</strong> identity, ambition, loss\n   639→                </p>\n   640→                \n   641→            </section>\n   642→            \n   643→\n   644→            \n   645→            <section>\n   646→                <h2>🤖 Analysis Models</h2>\n   647→                <table style=\"width: 100%; margin-top: 1rem; border-collapse: collapse;\">\n   648→                    <thead>\n   649→                        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid var(--primary);\">\n   650→                            <th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted);\">Phase</th>\n   651→                            <th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted);\">Model