{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PZmczR9xo6MnWSMwUvBbeW","type":"tool_result","content":"   777→        <div id=\"chapters\" class=\"tab-content\">\n   778→        <section>\n   779→            <h2>📑 Chapter Guide</h2>\n   780→\n   781→            \n   782→\n   783→            <h3 style=\"margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-soft);\">Chapters</h3>\n   784→            \n   785→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   786→                <h4>\n   787→                    <span>Chapter 1</span>\n   788→                    <span class=\"meta\">5,441 words &middot; 36m</span>\n   789→                </h4>\n   790→                \n   791→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with Robert Walton preparing for an Arctic expedition from Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a courageous English lieutenant and a gentle master, while reflecting on his solitary upbringing, self-education through voyage literature and poetry, and grueling training with whale fishermen under extreme cold, famine, and sleep deprivation; his ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure driving a dog-drawn sledge across the frozen expanse, and two days later discovers a second adrift sledge carrying a nearly frozen European stranger who reveals he pursued a 'dæmon' across the ice, leading Walton to nurse him back to health with brandy and warmth, during which the stranger, marked by profound grief, transcendent appreciation for nature, and piercing intellect, expresses his irrevocable fate and begs Walton to hear his tragic tale as a warning against obsessive ambition, prompting Walton to resolve to record the stranger’s narrative in full each night.</div>\n   792→                \n   793→                \n   794→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   795→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   796→                    \n   797→                    <span class=\"tag\">Robert Walton</span>\n   798→                    \n   799→                    <span class=\"tag\">Margaret Saville</span>\n   800→                    \n   801→                    <span class=\"tag\">Uncle Thomas</span>\n   802→                    \n   803→                    <span class=\"tag\">The lieutenant</span>\n   804→                    \n   805→                    <span class=\"tag\">The master</span>\n   806→                    \n   807→                    <span class=\"tag\">The dæmon</span>\n   808→                    \n   809→                    <span class=\"tag\">The stranger</span>\n   810→                    \n   811→                    \n   812→                </div>\n   813→                \n   814→            </div>\n   815→            \n   816→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   817→                <h4>\n   818→                    <span>Chapter 2</span>\n   819→                    <span class=\"meta\">1,766 words &middot; 11m</span>\n   820→                </h4>\n   821→                \n   822→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter recounts the noble lineage of the narrator’s family in Geneva, detailing how his father, a respected public servant, devotedly sought out his impoverished friend Beaufort after his fall from wealth, eventually finding him dying in a humble cottage near Lucerne. Beaufort’s daughter, Caroline, endured extreme hardship caring for him until his death, surviving through menial labor like plaiting straw, until the narrator’s father discovered her grieving at his coffin and took her under his protection. Two years later, the father married Caroline, and their deep, reverent bond led them to travel through Italy and France, where the narrator was born in Naples and raised as their only child until age five. During a visit to the Lake of Como, they encountered a destitute peasant family nurturing a fair-haired orphan girl, Elizabeth Lavenza, whose ethereal beauty and noble bearing moved the narrator’s mother to adopt her, bringing her home as a cherished companion and 'more than sister' to the narrator, who viewed her as a divine gift and his lifelong charge.</div>\n   823→                \n   824→                \n   825→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   826→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   827→                    \n   828→                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator (Victor)</span>\n   829→                    \n   830→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n   831→                    \n   832→                    <span class=\"tag\">Mother (Caroline Beaufort)</span>\n   833→                    \n   834→                    <span class=\"tag\">Beaufort</span>\n   835→                    \n   836→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth Lavenza</span>\n   837→                    \n   838→                    <span class=\"tag\">Peasant woman</span>\n   839→                    \n   840→                    <span class=\"tag\">Peasant man</span>\n   841→                    \n   842→                    \n   843→                </div>\n   844→                \n   845→            </div>\n   846→            \n   847→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   848→                <h4>\n   849→                    <span>Chapter 3</span>\n   850→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,206 words &middot; 14m</span>\n   851→                </h4>\n   852→                \n   853→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter reflects on the narrator's idyllic childhood in Geneva, where he grew up alongside Elizabeth, whose gentle nature softened his intense passions, and Henry Clerval, whose romantic ideals complemented his own intellectual fervor. At thirteen, he discovered Cornelius Agrippa’s works during a storm-bound stay at an inn near Thonon, becoming obsessed with alchemy and the secrets of nature, despite his father’s dismissive remark. His devotion to these outdated theories deepened as he studied Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus, driven by a desire to unlock life’s deepest mysteries, including the elixir of life and the raising of spirits. At fifteen, a violent thunderstorm near Belrive destroyed an oak tree with mysterious force, and a natural philosopher’s explanation of electricity and galvanism shattered his faith in his previous studies, leading him to abandon alchemy for mathematics. Though he experienced temporary peace and clarity, he acknowledges this shift as a futile attempt to evade his destined path toward ruin.</div>\n   854→                \n   855→                \n   856→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   857→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   858→                    \n   859→                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator (Victor)</span>\n   860→                    \n   861→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n   862→                    \n   863→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n   864→                    \n   865→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n   866→                    \n   867→                    \n   868→                </div>\n   869→                \n   870→            </div>\n   871→            \n   872→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   873→                <h4>\n   874→                    <span>Chapter 4</span>\n   875→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,675 words &middot; 17m</span>\n   876→                </h4>\n   877→                \n   878→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with the narrator, Victor Frankenstein, preparing to depart for the University of Ingolstadt after the death of his mother, who succumbed to scarlet fever while nursing Elizabeth. His departure, delayed by mourning, is marked by emotional farewells with his father, Elizabeth, and his close friend Henry Clerval, who is forbidden by his merchant father from accompanying him. Victor travels by carriage to Ingolstadt, a long and fatiguing journey during which he reflects on his isolation and reluctance to face strangers. Upon arrival, he meets Professor Krempe, who harshly dismisses his previous studies in alchemy as obsolete, leaving Victor unmoved but unimpressed. Later, he attends a lecture by Professor Waldman, whose passionate praise of modern chemistry ignites a profound transformation in Victor, awakening a consuming ambition to surpass existing scientific boundaries and uncover the deepest mysteries of life.</div>\n   879→                \n   880→                \n   881→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   882→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   883→                    \n   884→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   885→                    \n   886→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n   887→                    \n   888→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n   889→                    \n   890→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n   891→                    \n   892→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Krempe</span>\n   893→                    \n   894→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Waldman</span>\n   895→                    \n   896→                    \n   897→                </div>\n   898→                \n   899→            </div>\n   900→            \n   901→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   902→                <h4>\n   903→                    <span>Chapter 5</span>\n   904→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,535 words &middot; 16m</span>\n   905→                </h4>\n   906→                \n   907→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter details Victor Frankenstein's obsessive two-year pursuit of natural philosophy and chemistry at the University of Ingolstadt, where he is mentored by the kind Professor Waldman and the blunt Professor Krempe. Driven by a fascination with the origin of life, Victor shifts his focus to anatomy and the decay of the human body, spending nights in charnel-houses and dissecting rooms to collect materials. His single-minded obsession leads him to construct a gigantic eight-foot-tall creature, working in secret in a isolated chamber at the top of his house, fueled by relentless energy and a belief that he is on the verge of a revolutionary scientific breakthrough. As summer turns to winter and his health deteriorates, he becomes physically emaciated, mentally tormented, and socially isolated, ignoring letters from his family and losing all connection to the natural world around him.</div>\n   908→                \n   909→                \n   910→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   911→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   912→                    \n   913→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   914→                    \n   915→                    <span class=\"tag\">Professor Krempe</span>\n   916→                    \n   917→                    <span class=\"tag\">Professor Waldman</span>\n   918→                    \n   919→                    \n   920→                </div>\n   921→                \n   922→            </div>\n   923→            \n   924→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   925→                <h4>\n   926→                    <span>Chapter 6</span>\n   927→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,357 words &middot; 15m</span>\n   928→                </h4>\n   929→                \n   930→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Victor Frankenstein in the aftermath of creating a living being, overwhelmed by horror and disgust at its grotesque appearance, leading him to flee his laboratory in the early hours of a dreary November morning. He spends the night in anguish, tormented by nightmares and the terrifying sight of the creature looming over his bed, before escaping into the rain-soaked courtyard of his college in Ingolstadt. At dawn, he wanders the streets in a state of psychological collapse, haunted by the creature’s presence, until he is unexpectedly reunited with his childhood friend Henry Clerval, who arrives by Swiss diligence. Victor’s emotional relief is short-lived; his mental state deteriorates rapidly, culminating in a violent fit when he believes the creature has returned, triggering a severe nervous fever that confines him to bed for months, during which Henry becomes his sole, devoted nurse.</div>\n   931→                \n   932→                \n   933→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   934→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   935→                    \n   936→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   937→                    \n   938→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n   939→                    \n   940→                    \n   941→                </div>\n   942→                \n   943→            </div>\n   944→            \n   945→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   946→                <h4>\n   947→                    <span>Chapter 7</span>\n   948→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,718 words &middot; 18m</span>\n   949→                </h4>\n   950→                \n   951→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Victor receiving a heartfelt letter from Elizabeth, who expresses deep concern for his health and longs for his return to Geneva, describing the unchanged beauty of their home, the growth of Ernest and William, and the return of Justine Moritz after her mother’s death. Victor, moved and fatigued, writes a reply, marking a step in his convalescence. He then accompanies Clerval in introducing himself to the university professors, where he suffers intense emotional distress at the mention of natural philosophy—his former passion now tied to trauma—reacting with silent agony to Waldman’s praise and Krempe’s blunt congratulations. Clerval, attuned to Victor’s suffering, redirects conversations and shares his own studies in Oriental languages, which Victor embraces as a soothing distraction from his past. The two spend a cheerful winter together, and in spring, they embark on a two-week pedestrian tour of the Ingolstadt countryside, during which Victor’s spirits are restored by nature, friendship, and Clerval’s imaginative tales and poetic recitations, culminating in a joyful return to college on a sunny Sunday afternoon amid dancing peasants and a renewed sense of happiness.</div>\n   952→                \n   953→                \n   954→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   955→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   956→                    \n   957→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   958→                    \n   959→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n   960→                    \n   961→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth Lavenza</span>\n   962→                    \n   963→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Waldman</span>\n   964→                    \n   965→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Krempe</span>\n   966→                    \n   967→                    <span class=\"tag\">Justine Moritz</span>\n   968→                    \n   969→                    <span class=\"tag\">Ernest Frankenstein</span>\n   970→                    \n   971→                    <span class=\"tag\">William Frankenstein</span>\n   972→                    \n   973→                    \n   974→                    <span class=\"tag\">+2 more</span>\n   975→                    \n   976→                </div>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"150a4e22-b84e-461f-8beb-baf95e8638e0","uuid":"45333055-3ff6-4f95-9c4d-7756122cb9e7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/output/frankenstein/report.html","content":"        <div id=\"chapters\" class=\"tab-content\">\n        <section>\n            <h2>📑 Chapter Guide</h2>\n\n            \n\n            <h3 style=\"margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-soft);\">Chapters</h3>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 1</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">5,441 words &middot; 36m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with Robert Walton preparing for an Arctic expedition from Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a courageous English lieutenant and a gentle master, while reflecting on his solitary upbringing, self-education through voyage literature and poetry, and grueling training with whale fishermen under extreme cold, famine, and sleep deprivation; his ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure driving a dog-drawn sledge across the frozen expanse, and two days later discovers a second adrift sledge carrying a nearly frozen European stranger who reveals he pursued a 'dæmon' across the ice, leading Walton to nurse him back to health with brandy and warmth, during which the stranger, marked by profound grief, transcendent appreciation for nature, and piercing intellect, expresses his irrevocable fate and begs Walton to hear his tragic tale as a warning against obsessive ambition, prompting Walton to resolve to record the stranger’s narrative in full each night.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Robert Walton</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Margaret Saville</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Uncle Thomas</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">The lieutenant</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">The master</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">The dæmon</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">The stranger</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 2</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">1,766 words &middot; 11m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter recounts the noble lineage of the narrator’s family in Geneva, detailing how his father, a respected public servant, devotedly sought out his impoverished friend Beaufort after his fall from wealth, eventually finding him dying in a humble cottage near Lucerne. Beaufort’s daughter, Caroline, endured extreme hardship caring for him until his death, surviving through menial labor like plaiting straw, until the narrator’s father discovered her grieving at his coffin and took her under his protection. Two years later, the father married Caroline, and their deep, reverent bond led them to travel through Italy and France, where the narrator was born in Naples and raised as their only child until age five. During a visit to the Lake of Como, they encountered a destitute peasant family nurturing a fair-haired orphan girl, Elizabeth Lavenza, whose ethereal beauty and noble bearing moved the narrator’s mother to adopt her, bringing her home as a cherished companion and 'more than sister' to the narrator, who viewed her as a divine gift and his lifelong charge.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator (Victor)</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Mother (Caroline Beaufort)</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Beaufort</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth Lavenza</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Peasant woman</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Peasant man</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 3</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">2,206 words &middot; 14m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter reflects on the narrator's idyllic childhood in Geneva, where he grew up alongside Elizabeth, whose gentle nature softened his intense passions, and Henry Clerval, whose romantic ideals complemented his own intellectual fervor. At thirteen, he discovered Cornelius Agrippa’s works during a storm-bound stay at an inn near Thonon, becoming obsessed with alchemy and the secrets of nature, despite his father’s dismissive remark. His devotion to these outdated theories deepened as he studied Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus, driven by a desire to unlock life’s deepest mysteries, including the elixir of life and the raising of spirits. At fifteen, a violent thunderstorm near Belrive destroyed an oak tree with mysterious force, and a natural philosopher’s explanation of electricity and galvanism shattered his faith in his previous studies, leading him to abandon alchemy for mathematics. Though he experienced temporary peace and clarity, he acknowledges this shift as a futile attempt to evade his destined path toward ruin.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator (Victor)</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 4</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">2,675 words &middot; 17m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with the narrator, Victor Frankenstein, preparing to depart for the University of Ingolstadt after the death of his mother, who succumbed to scarlet fever while nursing Elizabeth. His departure, delayed by mourning, is marked by emotional farewells with his father, Elizabeth, and his close friend Henry Clerval, who is forbidden by his merchant father from accompanying him. Victor travels by carriage to Ingolstadt, a long and fatiguing journey during which he reflects on his isolation and reluctance to face strangers. Upon arrival, he meets Professor Krempe, who harshly dismisses his previous studies in alchemy as obsolete, leaving Victor unmoved but unimpressed. Later, he attends a lecture by Professor Waldman, whose passionate praise of modern chemistry ignites a profound transformation in Victor, awakening a consuming ambition to surpass existing scientific boundaries and uncover the deepest mysteries of life.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Krempe</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Waldman</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 5</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">2,535 words &middot; 16m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter details Victor Frankenstein's obsessive two-year pursuit of natural philosophy and chemistry at the University of Ingolstadt, where he is mentored by the kind Professor Waldman and the blunt Professor Krempe. Driven by a fascination with the origin of life, Victor shifts his focus to anatomy and the decay of the human body, spending nights in charnel-houses and dissecting rooms to collect materials. His single-minded obsession leads him to construct a gigantic eight-foot-tall creature, working in secret in a isolated chamber at the top of his house, fueled by relentless energy and a belief that he is on the verge of a revolutionary scientific breakthrough. As summer turns to winter and his health deteriorates, he becomes physically emaciated, mentally tormented, and socially isolated, ignoring letters from his family and losing all connection to the natural world around him.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Professor Krempe</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Professor Waldman</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 6</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">2,357 words &middot; 15m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Victor Frankenstein in the aftermath of creating a living being, overwhelmed by horror and disgust at its grotesque appearance, leading him to flee his laboratory in the early hours of a dreary November morning. He spends the night in anguish, tormented by nightmares and the terrifying sight of the creature looming over his bed, before escaping into the rain-soaked courtyard of his college in Ingolstadt. At dawn, he wanders the streets in a state of psychological collapse, haunted by the creature’s presence, until he is unexpectedly reunited with his childhood friend Henry Clerval, who arrives by Swiss diligence. Victor’s emotional relief is short-lived; his mental state deteriorates rapidly, culminating in a violent fit when he believes the creature has returned, triggering a severe nervous fever that confines him to bed for months, during which Henry becomes his sole, devoted nurse.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 7</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">2,718 words &middot; 18m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Victor receiving a heartfelt letter from Elizabeth, who expresses deep concern for his health and longs for his return to Geneva, describing the unchanged beauty of their home, the growth of Ernest and William, and the return of Justine Moritz after her mother’s death. Victor, moved and fatigued, writes a reply, marking a step in his convalescence. He then accompanies Clerval in introducing himself to the university professors, where he suffers intense emotional distress at the mention of natural philosophy—his former passion now tied to trauma—reacting with silent agony to Waldman’s praise and Krempe’s blunt congratulations. Clerval, attuned to Victor’s suffering, redirects conversations and shares his own studies in Oriental languages, which Victor embraces as a soothing distraction from his past. The two spend a cheerful winter together, and in spring, they embark on a two-week pedestrian tour of the Ingolstadt countryside, during which Victor’s spirits are restored by nature, friendship, and Clerval’s imaginative tales and poetic recitations, culminating in a joyful return to college on a sunny Sunday afternoon amid dancing peasants and a renewed sense of happiness.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth Lavenza</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Waldman</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Krempe</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Justine Moritz</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Ernest Frankenstein</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">William Frankenstein</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+2 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>","numLines":200,"startLine":777,"totalLines":26571}}}
