{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PdWUoUTRaNVRShNt9n37AT","type":"tool_result","content":"The wedding to Elizabeth, a union my creator undertakes in desperate hope to end his torment, becomes the final tragedy. On their wedding night, the creature murders Elizabeth, leaving my creator shattered and consumed by grief. His father dies of sorrow, and he is falsely accused of murdering Clerval, who was killed by the creature in Ireland. After being exonerated, he pursues the monster across Europe and into the Arctic, driven by a vow of vengeance. In the frozen wastes, he finally catches sight of the creature’s sledge, but a seismic rift separates them. As he drifts on a melting ice floe, he is rescued by Robert Walton’s expedition. Before dying, my creator implores Walton to continue the hunt, recounting his entire story in a final act of warning. The creature appears at the end, remorseful and broken, confessing his descent into evil due to isolation and rejection, and then vanishes into the ice, ending his own life on a pyre he has built.</div>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with Robert Walton preparing for an Arctic expedition from Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a courageous English lieutenant and a gentle master, while reflecting on his solitary upbringing, self-education through voyage literature and poetry, and grueling training with whale fishermen under extreme cold, famine, and sleep deprivation; his ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure driving a dog-drawn sledge across the frozen expanse, and two days later discovers a second adrift sledge carrying a nearly frozen European stranger who reveals he pursued a 'dæmon' across the ice, leading Walton to nurse him back to health with brandy and warmth, during which the stranger, marked by profound grief, transcendent appreciation for nature, and piercing intellect, expresses his irrevocable fate and begs Walton to hear his tragic tale as a warning against obsessive ambition, prompting Walton to resolve to record the stranger’s narrative in full each night.</div>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Robert Walton</span>\n                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Margaret is a deeply cherished sister and emotional anchor to her brother, Robert Walton, whose journey to the North Pole is documented through letters to her. She is portrayed as a source of moral support, love, and compassion, with Walton expressing profound gratitude for her kindness and affection. Though she remains offstage throughout the narrative, her presence is felt as a stabilizing, benevolent force, and her absence is a source of emotional weight for Walton, who longs for a confidant during his trials. Her role underscores themes of familial devotion and the human need for connection.</div>\n                                <div class=\"description\">Mrs. Saville is Robert Walton's beloved sister, deeply cherished and emotionally significant to him throughout his journey. She is portrayed as a source of comfort, affection, and moral support, with ...</div>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"150a4e22-b84e-461f-8beb-baf95e8638e0","uuid":"d6f543ed-e877-463b-9cfc-e97dd1719a3b","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"The wedding to Elizabeth, a union my creator undertakes in desperate hope to end his torment, becomes the final tragedy. On their wedding night, the creature murders Elizabeth, leaving my creator shattered and consumed by grief. His father dies of sorrow, and he is falsely accused of murdering Clerval, who was killed by the creature in Ireland. After being exonerated, he pursues the monster across Europe and into the Arctic, driven by a vow of vengeance. In the frozen wastes, he finally catches sight of the creature’s sledge, but a seismic rift separates them. As he drifts on a melting ice