{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0192criXb8gXuJ4FqT2kjeoe","type":"tool_result","content":"     0→{\n     1→  \"metadata\": {\n     2→    \"title\": \"Contents\",\n     3→    \"author\": \"Mary Wollstonecraft (Godwin) Shelley\",\n     4→    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/Frankenstein_ebook.txt\",\n     5→    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n     6→    \"total_word_count\": 75060,\n     7→    \"total_character_count\": 418299,\n     8→    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 499.2266666666667,\n     9→    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n    10→  },\n    11→  \"structure\": [\n    12→    {\n    13→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    14→      \"title\": null,\n    15→      \"index\": 1,\n    16→      \"start_position\": 1000,\n    17→      \"end_position\": 31434,\n    18→      \"word_count\": 5441,\n    19→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 36.27333333333333,\n    20→      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n    21→      \"children\": [],\n    22→      \"characters_present\": [\n    23→        \"Robert Walton\",\n    24→        \"Margaret Saville\",\n    25→        \"Uncle Thomas\",\n    26→        \"The lieutenant\",\n    27→        \"The master\",\n    28→        \"The dæmon\",\n    29→        \"The stranger\"\n    30→      ],\n    31→      \"summary\": \"The chapter begins with Robert Walton preparing for an Arctic expedition from Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a courageous English lieutenant and a gentle master, while reflecting on his solitary upbringing, self-education through voyage literature and poetry, and grueling training with whale fishermen under extreme cold, famine, and sleep deprivation; his ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure driving a dog-drawn sledge across the frozen expanse, and two days later discovers a second adrift sledge carrying a nearly frozen European stranger who reveals he pursued a 'dæmon' across the ice, leading Walton to nurse him back to health with brandy and warmth, during which the stranger, marked by profound grief, transcendent appreciation for nature, and piercing intellect, expresses his irrevocable fate and begs Walton to hear his tragic tale as a warning against obsessive ambition, prompting Walton to resolve to record the stranger’s narrative in full each night.\"\n    32→    },\n    33→    {\n    34→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    35→      \"title\": null,\n    36→      \"index\": 2,\n    37→      \"start_position\": 31434,\n    38→      \"end_position\": 41575,\n    39→      \"word_count\": 1766,\n    40→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 11.773333333333333,\n    41→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    42→      \"children\": [],\n    43→      \"characters_present\": [\n    44→        \"Narrator (Victor)\",\n    45→        \"Father\",\n    46→        \"Mother (Caroline Beaufort)\",\n    47→        \"Beaufort\",\n    48→        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n    49→        \"Peasant woman\",\n    50→        \"Peasant man\"\n    51→      ],\n    52→      \"summary\": \"The chapter recounts the noble lineage of the narrator’s family in Geneva, detailing how his father, a respected public servant, devotedly sought out his impoverished friend Beaufort after his fall from wealth, eventually finding him dying in a humble cottage near Lucerne. Beaufort’s daughter, Caroline, endured extreme hardship caring for him until his death, surviving through menial labor like plaiting straw, until the narrator’s father discovered her grieving at his coffin and took her under his protection. Two years later, the father married Caroline, and their deep, reverent bond led them to travel through Italy and France, where the narrator was born in Naples and raised as their only child until age five. During a visit to the Lake of Como, they encountered a destitute peasant family nurturing a fair-haired orphan girl, Elizabeth Lavenza, whose ethereal beauty and noble bearing moved the narrator’s mother to adopt her, bringing her home as a cherished companion and 'more than sister' to the narrator, who viewed her as a divine gift and his lifelong charge.\"\n    53→    },\n    54→    {\n    55→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    56→      \"title\": null,\n    57→      \"index\": 3,\n    58→      \"start_position\": 41575,\n    59→      \"end_position\": 54263,\n    60→      \"word_count\": 2206,\n    61→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 14.706666666666667,\n    62→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    63→      \"children\": [],\n    64→      \"characters_present\": [\n    65→        \"Narrator (Victor)\",\n    66→        \"Elizabeth\",\n    67→        \"Henry Clerval\",\n    68→        \"Father\"\n    69→      ],\n    70→      \"summary\": \"The chapter reflects on the narrator's idyllic childhood in Geneva, where he grew up alongside Elizabeth, whose gentle nature softened his intense passions, and Henry Clerval, whose romantic ideals complemented his own intellectual fervor. At thirteen, he discovered Cornelius Agrippa’s works during a storm-bound stay at an inn near Thonon, becoming obsessed with alchemy and the secrets of nature, despite his father’s dismissive remark. His devotion to these outdated theories deepened as he studied Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus, driven by a desire to unlock life’s deepest mysteries, including the elixir of life and the raising of spirits. At fifteen, a violent thunderstorm near Belrive destroyed an oak tree with mysterious force, and a natural philosopher’s explanation of electricity and galvanism shattered his faith in his previous studies, leading him to abandon alchemy for mathematics. Though he experienced temporary peace and clarity, he acknowledges this shift as a futile attempt to evade his destined path toward ruin.\"\n    71→    },\n    72→    {\n    73→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    74→      \"title\": null,\n    75→      \"index\": 4,\n    76→      \"start_position\": 54263,\n    77→      \"end_position\": 69512,\n    78→      \"word_count\": 2675,\n    79→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 17.833333333333332,\n    80→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    81→      \"children\": [],\n    82→      \"characters_present\": [\n    83→        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n    84→        \"Elizabeth\",\n    85→        \"Father\",\n    86→        \"Henry Clerval\",\n    87→        \"M. Krempe\",\n    88→        \"M. Waldman\"\n    89→      ],\n    90→      \"summary\": \"The chapter begins with the narrator, Victor Frankenstein, preparing to depart for the University of Ingolstadt after the death of his mother, who succumbed to scarlet fever while nursing Elizabeth. His departure, delayed by mourning, is marked by emotional farewells with his father, Elizabeth, and his close friend Henry Clerval, who is forbidden by his merchant father from accompanying him. Victor travels by carriage to Ingolstadt, a long and fatiguing journey during which he reflects on his isolation and reluctance to face strangers. Upon arrival, he meets Professor Krempe, who harshly dismisses his previous studies in alchemy as obsolete, leaving Victor unmoved but unimpressed. Later, he attends a lecture by Professor Waldman, whose passionate praise of modern chemistry ignites a profound transformation in Victor, awakening a consuming ambition to surpass existing scientific boundaries and uncover the deepest mysteries of life.\"\n    91→    },\n    92→    {\n    93→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    94→      \"title\": null,\n    95→      \"index\": 5,\n    96→      \"start_position\": 69512,\n    97→      \"end_position\": 83926,\n    98→      \"word_count\": 2535,\n    99→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 16.9,\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write repo